UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Healthspan vs Lifespan: The Real Goal$v$, subtitle = $v$Why living longer is the wrong target, and being capable at 80 is the right one$v$, summary = $v$Average lifespan rose for a century and the years spent sick rose with it. The goal of this shelf is not more years. It is compressing decline into the smallest window at the very end.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Longevity reframed from fear of death to training for capability$v$, $v$The marginal decade, the most useful planning tool on the shelf$v$, $v$The honest frame every later lesson builds on$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Decline slopes are population averages; your own slope depends on what you train, which is the point$v$, $v$Not medical advice; a diagnosed condition changes the plan with a clinician$v$], body_md = $v$**The wrong picture**
Ask people whether they want to live to 100 and most hesitate, because they picture the wrong thing: a decade of frailty, dependence and waiting rooms. That picture is not paranoia. Medicine became very good at keeping you alive with chronic disease and mediocre at keeping you capable without it. So this shelf does not chase lifespan. It chases healthspan: the years you live strong, mobile, sharp and independent.

**Compression of morbidity**
Fries (1980) proposed the idea this whole shelf rests on: the goal is to compress morbidity, to squeeze the sick, declining phase of life into the shortest window right before death. Two people can both die at 88. One spends the last twenty years managing decline; the other hikes at 80 and fades over months. Same lifespan, radically different lives. Olshansky (2018) makes the same case in the language of public health.

**The marginal decade**
The planning tool: your marginal decade is the last decade of your life, and whatever you can do in it, you can only do if you trained for it. Capability declines on a slope. VO₂max falls roughly 10% a decade after 30, faster after 60 if untrained, and muscle mass follows a similar slide. You cannot start the climb in the marginal decade. You can only arrive with reserves.

So work backwards. Carry your own luggage at 80? Lift a grandchild at 75? Walk a fell at 85? Each has a strength and VO₂max price, and because the decline compounds, the version of you at 40 or 50 must be far stronger and fitter than the target, not slightly.

**The athlete's edge, and trap**
If you train, you are already building the reserve. The trap is training only for this summer, chasing a number on the bar or the scale while ignoring the capacities that decide the last twenty years. The next nine lessons rank those capacities by the strength of the mortality evidence: hazard ratios first, no folklore, no supplement hype.

**The honesty contract**
Longevity is the most hype-polluted corner of health. Every lesson carries an evidence chip: strong means repeated large human studies or meta-analyses on the practice as written; promising means good but incomplete evidence; speculative means mechanism without outcomes. When the evidence does not support a popular claim, the lesson says so by name. You are not training to avoid death. You are training so the last decade still belongs to you.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Every check-in, streak and session in this app compounds over decades. This lesson says what it compounds toward: not more years, but more capable years, planned backwards from the marginal decade.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Write down three physical things you want to still do in your 80s.$v$, $v$For each, name the capacity it needs: strength, endurance, balance. That is your training why.$v$, $v$Read lesson 2 tomorrow with the list to hand.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Healthspan, capable years, is the target, not lifespan.$v$, $v$Compression of morbidity: squeeze decline into the smallest window at the end.$v$, $v$Plan backwards from the marginal decade; the reserves you need at 80 are built decades earlier.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What does compression of morbidity mean?", "choices": ["Squeezing the sick, declining phase into the shortest window at the end of life", "Avoiding all illness forever", "Living as long as possible at any cost"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Fries 1980: the win is not more total years, it is fewer years spent in decline."}, {"q": "Why must the reserves for the marginal decade be built decades earlier?", "choices": ["Gyms have age limits", "VO₂max and muscle decline on a compounding slope you cannot reverse from zero at 80", "Insurance requires it"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Capability falls roughly 10% a decade after 30. You arrive in the last decade with whatever you built."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Fries, J. F.", "title": "Aging, natural death, and the compression of morbidity (N. Engl. J. Med.)", "year": 1980}, {"author": "Olshansky, S. J.", "title": "From lifespan to healthspan (JAMA)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Attia, P.", "title": "Outlive: The Science and Art of Longevity", "year": 2023}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What do you want to be able to do at 80, and what would the version of you at 50 need to be able to do to get there?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You wrote three things for your 80s. Which capacity did you train this week, and which did you not?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 1, display_order = 1, course_role = $v$foundations$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'healthspan-vs-lifespan';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$The Hierarchy of Levers$v$, subtitle = $v$What moves the needle, ranked by mortality data rather than marketing budgets$v$, summary = $v$Longevity content is mostly noise about the last one percent of gains. Here is the honest hierarchy: five levers with large human mortality data, and why everything else is a rounding error until these are handled.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 min once", "frequency": "Rate yourself once; re-rate quarterly", "intensity": "Five levers, one to five each; the lowest score is the training ground", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$One ranked map for the whole shelf$v$, $v$Protection from spending money at the bottom of the pyramid$v$, $v$Each lever linked to its own lesson$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The ranking is by evidence size, not by your situation; a clinician's diagnosis outranks it$v$, $v$Cohort data throughout; the levers are the best-supported bets, not guarantees$v$], body_md = $v$**Where the airtime goes**
Open any longevity feed and you will see ice baths, NAD drips, glucose monitors and thirty-ingredient powders. Meanwhile the five levers with the largest human mortality datasets get a fraction of the attention, because nobody profits when you go for a run. Here is the hierarchy this shelf follows, ranked by the size and consistency of the evidence.

**Lever 1: cardiorespiratory fitness**
Mandsager et al. (2018) tested 122,007 patients on a treadmill and followed them for years: low fitness carried a mortality risk comparable to or worse than smoking, diabetes or coronary disease, and the benefit kept climbing with fitness with no ceiling found. The least fit died at about five times the rate of the fittest. No drug, food or supplement comes close. Lesson 3.

**Lever 2: strength and muscle**
Grip strength predicted death in 139,691 people across 17 countries in the PURE study, more strongly than systolic blood pressure (Leong et al. 2015). Muscle is the armour against falls, frailty and metabolic disease. Lesson 4.

**Lever 3: sleep**
Habitually sleeping under six hours tracks with about 12% higher all-cause mortality in meta-analysis, and mid-life short sleep with later dementia. Repair happens in sleep; you cannot out-train its absence. Lesson 6.

**Lever 4: metabolic health**
Insulin resistance sits upstream of the biggest killers: heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and much of dementia risk. It is measurable with a tape, and it responds within weeks to the levers above plus food. Lessons 5 and 7.

**Lever 5: connection and purpose**
The least marketable lever with hard data: social isolation carries mortality risk in the same class as light smoking across 308,849 people (Holt-Lunstad et al. 2010). Lesson 8.

**How big is the pyramid**
Li et al. (2018) followed more than 120,000 Americans and found that five low-tech factors, not smoking, a healthy weight, regular activity, a decent diet and moderate alcohol, predicted 12 to 14 extra years of life expectancy at 50. That is the size of the prize in the basics.

**What the evidence does not support**
No supplement, gadget or hack has mortality evidence within an order of magnitude of any lever above. Optimising a lab number while sleeping five hours is rearranging deck chairs. Lesson 9 walks the graveyard by name.

**The rule for the rest of the shelf**
Before adopting anything new, ask which lever it pulls, and how hard. If the answer is none of the five, it is entertainment, not longevity.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Attention is the scarcest resource. Fitness, strength, sleep, metabolic health and connection are where decades of mortality data say to spend it, and the standard every new hack is measured against.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Rate yourself from one to five on each lever. The lowest score is the highest-return training ground.$v$, $v$Unfollow one account that sells bottom-of-pyramid hacks; the attention belongs to your weakest lever.$v$, $v$Open the lesson for that lever next.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Five levers with large mortality data: fitness, strength, sleep, metabolic health, connection.$v$, $v$Low fitness rivals smoking as a mortality risk in 122,007 patients.$v$, $v$Five basic lifestyle factors predict 12 to 14 extra years at 50; the basics are the whole prize.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Which lever tops the hierarchy by mortality evidence?", "choices": ["Cold exposure", "Supplement optimisation", "Cardiorespiratory fitness"], "correct": 2, "explain": "In 122,007 patients, low fitness rivalled or exceeded smoking and diabetes as a mortality risk, with no ceiling on the benefit."}, {"q": "How much extra life expectancy did five basic factors predict at 50?", "choices": ["Twelve to fourteen years", "Thirty years", "Two to three years"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Li 2018: not smoking, a healthy weight, activity, diet and moderate alcohol. The basics carry the bulk of the prize."}, {"q": "A new gadget claims longevity benefits. The first question?", "choices": ["How many followers has the founder", "Which of the five levers does it pull, and how hard", "Is it expensive enough to work"], "correct": 1, "explain": "If it does not pull fitness, strength, sleep, metabolic health or connection, it is entertainment."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Mandsager, K. et al.", "title": "Association of cardiorespiratory fitness with long-term mortality among adults undergoing exercise treadmill testing (JAMA Netw. Open)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Leong, D. P. et al.", "title": "Prognostic value of grip strength: findings from the Prospective Urban Rural Epidemiology (PURE) study (Lancet)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Li, Y. et al.", "title": "Impact of healthy lifestyle factors on life expectancies in the US population (Circulation)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Holt-Lunstad, J., Smith, T. B. & Layton, J. B.", "title": "Social relationships and mortality risk: a meta-analytic review (PLoS Med.)", "year": 2010}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of the five levers do you spend the most money on, and which the most time?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You rated the five and picked the lowest. What did you do about it this week?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 2, display_order = 2, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'hierarchy-of-longevity-levers';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$VO₂max: The Strongest Predictor$v$, subtitle = $v$The single number most tied to how long you live, and the weekly template that raises it$v$, summary = $v$Cardiorespiratory fitness predicts mortality better than smoking status, diabetes or blood pressure. It is also trainable at any age. Here is the weekly structure that moves it: a Zone 2 base and one hard interval session.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "45 to 60 min easy sessions; one 45 min hard session", "frequency": "2–4 Zone 2 sessions and one 4×4 a week", "intensity": "Conversational pace for the base; 90–95% of maximum heart rate for the intervals", "prerequisites": "4–6 weeks of Zone 2 before the first 4×4; no cardiac symptoms"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Targets the number with the strongest mortality association in exercise science$v$, $v$A simple weekly template$v$, $v$Fits around existing strength training$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The mortality data are cohorts; fitness is partly inherited, and the number overstates what training alone delivers$v$, $v$The 4×4 is near-maximal; a cardiologist first with heart disease or untreated hypertension$v$], body_md = $v$**The data**
If you could know one number about a stranger and bet on their lifespan, the evidence says pick VO₂max, the maximal rate at which the body uses oxygen. Mandsager et al. (2018) followed 122,007 treadmill-tested patients: each step up in fitness meant lower mortality, with the biggest single jump between the least fit and the merely below average, and no ceiling at the top. Kodama et al. (2009) pooled 102,980 people and put a number on it: every 1 MET of fitness, about 3.5 ml of oxygen per kilo per minute, associated with 13% lower all-cause mortality. These are cohorts, and fitness is partly inherited, so the size of the number overstates what training alone buys. The direction is not in doubt.

**Why it decides the marginal decade**
Everyday life has a fixed oxygen cost. Climbing stairs costs about 4 METs whether you are 30 or 85. VO₂max falls about 10% a decade after 30, so the question is where your slope started. A 50-year-old at 40 ml per kilo reaches 80 with room to spare; one at 26 reaches 80 unable to climb stairs without resting. Same slope, different starting altitude.

**The template**
Decades of endurance research converge on the same split, most volume easy and a small slice very hard (Seiler 2010). The base: Zone 2, 2–4 sessions a week of 45–60 minutes at a pace where you can speak in full sentences but could not sing. The Training shelf owns the mechanism and the dose; this is the same tool aimed at lifespan. The peak: one 4×4 a week, four minutes at 90–95% of maximum heart rate then three minutes easy, four times, after 4–6 weeks of base. Helgerud et al. (2007) used exactly this structure to raise VO₂max by 7.2% in eight weeks; the Training shelf has the session in full. One a week is enough; two is the ceiling.

**What the evidence does not support**
That hard cardio is dangerous for ordinary trainees, or that walking alone maximises the benefit. Walking is a superb floor, and the curve keeps paying above it; only harder work moves VO₂max once the beginner gains are spent.

**Progress markers**
No lab needed. Watch the pace at the same easy heart rate, the 4×4 interval pace, or the resting heart rate trending down. Log the sessions in the check-in; the streak is the programme.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Every 1 MET of fitness associates with about 13% lower all-cause mortality, the steepest risk-for-effort trade in health. One hard session and an easy base each week is the whole entry fee.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Schedule one 4×4 this week if you have the base, or one more Zone 2 session if you do not.$v$, $v$Do one 45-minute Zone 2 session today at true conversational pace.$v$, $v$Note your pace at the same easy heart rate; that is the marker.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$VO₂max is the strongest single mortality predictor in exercise science.$v$, $v$Every 1 MET of fitness is about 13% lower all-cause mortality in the pooled cohorts.$v$, $v$Template: 2–4 Zone 2 sessions and one 4×4 a week.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Roughly how much lower is all-cause mortality per 1 MET of fitness?", "choices": ["50%", "2%", "13%"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Kodama 2009, 102,980 people. Moving from unfit to average-fit is the biggest health trade there is."}, {"q": "What is the 4×4?", "choices": ["Four minutes at 90–95% of maximum heart rate, three minutes easy, four times", "Forty-four minutes of sprinting", "Four sets of four reps at maximum weight"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Helgerud's structure: 7.2% more VO₂max in eight weeks. One a week here, after the base."}, {"q": "Why does VO₂max decide late-life independence?", "choices": ["It does not; only genetics matter", "Daily tasks have a fixed oxygen cost and your reserve above it shrinks every decade", "High VO₂max prevents all disease"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Stairs cost about 4 METs at any age. The starting altitude decides whether they are trivial or impossible at 80."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Mandsager, K. et al.", "title": "Association of cardiorespiratory fitness with long-term mortality among adults undergoing exercise treadmill testing (JAMA Netw. Open)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Kodama, S. et al.", "title": "Cardiorespiratory fitness as a quantitative predictor of all-cause mortality and cardiovascular events in healthy men and women: a meta-analysis (JAMA)", "year": 2009}, {"author": "Seiler, S.", "title": "What is best practice for training intensity and duration distribution in endurance athletes? (Int. J. Sports Physiol. Perform.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Helgerud, J. et al.", "title": "Aerobic high-intensity intervals improve VO2max more than moderate training (Med. Sci. Sports Exerc.)", "year": 2007}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What can you do today at the same heart rate you could five years ago, and what can you not?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A month of the template. What happened to the pace at your easy heart rate?$v$, practice_minutes = 45, lesson_number = 3, display_order = 3, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'vo2max-strongest-predictor';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Strength: The Longevity Organ$v$, subtitle = $v$Grip strength out-predicts blood pressure; muscle is the account you draw on at 80$v$, summary = $v$After 50 you can lose one to two percent of muscle a year unless you fight for it. Strength predicts survival, prevents the falls that end independence, and responds to training at every age tested.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "45 min", "frequency": "2 full-body sessions a week", "intensity": "Two to four hard sets per pattern, two or three reps in reserve", "prerequisites": "Learn the hinge and the squat before loading them"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Grounded in the largest strength-mortality datasets$v$, $v$A minimum effective dose: two sessions, six patterns$v$, $v$Direct protection against the fall that ends most independent lives$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Load before technique is the injury path at any age$v$, $v$The mortality data are cohorts; the training trials are the part that is randomised$v$, $v$Supervised programmes are the safe route past 70 for the first months$v$], body_md = $v$**The grip finding**
Leong et al. (2015) followed 139,691 adults across 17 countries in the PURE study with a device that measures grip strength. Every 5 kg drop in grip associated with 16% higher all-cause mortality, which made grip a stronger predictor than systolic blood pressure. Nobody dies of weak hands. Grip is a window into total-body muscle, and muscle is a longevity organ.

**What muscle does besides move you**
It is the glucose sink: muscle clears most post-meal glucose, so more muscle means better insulin sensitivity and lever 4 handled at the root. It is the protein reserve: in illness or injury the body strips muscle to fuel immunity and repair, and the people who survive major illness are disproportionately the ones who walked in strong. It is fall insurance: after 65, a fall is the usual first step of the dependence spiral, fracture, hospital, deconditioning, and leg and hip power is what catches a stumble before it becomes a fall.

**Sarcopenia is mostly a choice**
Losing one to two percent of muscle a year after 50 is the untrained trajectory. Trained 70-year-olds routinely out-muscle sedentary 40-year-olds. Fiatarone et al. (1990) trained people in their nineties with supervised progressive resistance and got large strength gains. The adaptation machinery never fully closes; the cost of rebuilding rises with every decade you wait.

**The dose**
Momma et al. (2022) pooled the cohorts and found 30 to 60 minutes of muscle-strengthening work a week associated with 10 to 17% lower all-cause mortality. Two full-body sessions a week cover it, across six patterns: squat for the stairs and the chair at 85; hinge for picking things up safely forever; push; pull for posture and grip; carry for luggage, groceries and grandchildren; and a balance element, single-leg work, the fall-proofing multiplier. Two to four hard sets per pattern per week, with two or three reps left in the tank when the set ends. The Training shelf has the progression rule and a block to start with.

**What the evidence does not support**
That lifting is dangerous for older adults; supervised programmes show the opposite, and frailty is the danger. Or that endless light circuits build the reserve; muscle answers to progressive load, and the weight must creep up. Every session is a deposit in the account your 80-year-old self will live from.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Strength is the difference between a stumble and a hip fracture, between carrying your own life at 80 and asking permission. It predicts survival better than blood pressure, and two sessions a week keep it.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Count your week: which of the six patterns are missing? Add one to the next session.$v$, $v$Test yourself: rise from a chair five times without using the hands, timed; a validated frailty screen worth beating for life.$v$, $v$Write down the weight on one lift so next week it can go up.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Every 5 kg of grip lost is about 16% higher all-cause mortality across 139,691 people.$v$, $v$Muscle is a glucose sink, an illness reserve and fall insurance, not only movement.$v$, $v$Two full-body sessions a week across squat, hinge, push, pull, carry and balance.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What did the PURE study find about grip strength?", "choices": ["It cannot be trained after 60", "It only matters for athletes", "Each 5 kg less associated with 16% higher all-cause mortality, out-predicting blood pressure"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Grip is a proxy for total-body muscle, and muscle is a survival organ: glucose sink, illness reserve, fall insurance."}, {"q": "What weekly dose of strength work associated with 10 to 17% lower mortality?", "choices": ["30 to 60 minutes a week", "No association", "Daily two-hour sessions"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Momma 2022. Two focused full-body sessions cover it. The dose is smaller than most people fear."}, {"q": "Is meaningful strength gain still possible past 80?", "choices": ["No, the window closes at 60", "Yes; a trial in nonagenarians produced large gains", "Only with hormone therapy"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Fiatarone 1990 trained people in their nineties with supervised progressive resistance. The machinery stays open."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Leong, D. P. et al.", "title": "Prognostic value of grip strength: findings from the Prospective Urban Rural Epidemiology (PURE) study (Lancet)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Momma, H., Kawakami, R., Honda, T. & Sawada, S. S.", "title": "Muscle-strengthening activities are associated with lower risk and mortality in major non-communicable diseases: a systematic review and meta-analysis of cohort studies (Br. J. Sports Med.)", "year": 2022}, {"author": "Fiatarone, M. A. et al.", "title": "High-intensity strength training in nonagenarians: effects on skeletal muscle (JAMA)", "year": 1990}, {"author": "Srikanthan, P. & Karlamangla, A. S.", "title": "Muscle mass index as a predictor of longevity in older adults (Am. J. Med.)", "year": 2014}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of the six patterns have you not loaded in a month, and what in your life at 80 depends on it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Two weeks of two sessions. Which lift went up, and how did the chair test go?$v$, practice_minutes = 45, lesson_number = 4, display_order = 4, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'strength-longevity-organ';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Protein and the Aging Athlete$v$, subtitle = $v$Anabolic resistance means yesterday's intake stops working; here is the dose that keeps muscle$v$, summary = $v$The muscle from lesson 4 is built from dietary protein, and as you age the body responds less to each gram. The fix is a higher, evenly spread dose. The kidney fear, for healthy kidneys, is not supported.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "Daily", "frequency": "Three or four meals", "intensity": "1.6–2.2 g/kg/day; about 0.4 g/kg per meal, which matters more with age", "prerequisites": "Healthy kidney function; kidney disease sets its own dose with a clinician"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A concrete daily target you can apply today$v$, $v$Anabolic resistance explained, and why older muscle needs a bigger dose per meal$v$, $v$The kidney myth checked against randomised trials$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Existing kidney disease: the dose is a clinician's decision$v$, $v$Above 2.2 g/kg there is no further gain; the margin costs food budget, nothing else$v$], body_md = $v$**Age changes the rules**
Muscle is built and kept from one raw material, dietary protein, and with age the muscle responds less to each gram. Researchers call it anabolic resistance. Moore et al. (2015) quantified it: older muscle needs about 0.4 g/kg per meal to get the full building response, against about a quarter of a gram per kilo when young. The older you get, the more protein each meal must carry to send the same keep-the-muscle signal.

**The daily target**
For training adults who intend to keep muscle across decades, the evidence supports 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day, roughly double the old recommended intake. That older figure, 0.8 g per kilo, was set to prevent deficiency in sedentary people, not to preserve a longevity organ under training. Morton et al. (2018) pooled 49 trials and found the lean-mass benefit of extra protein plateauing near 1.6 g per kilo, with the margin above it costing nothing but food budget. For an 80 kg adult that is 130 to 175 g a day, and it will not happen by accident.

**Spread it**
Muscle protein synthesis is triggered per meal, not per day. Three or four meals of 30 to 50 g each beat the common pattern of 10 g at breakfast and 80 g at dinner, because low-protein meals, especially with anabolic resistance, never reach the trigger at all. Anchor each meal on the protein first; the Nutrition shelf has three breakfasts and a plate that do it.

**What the evidence does not support**
That high protein damages healthy kidneys. Devries et al. (2018) pooled randomised trials of higher against lower protein and found no harmful effect on kidney function in people with healthy kidneys. The caution is real for existing kidney disease, which is a clinician's conversation; for healthy trainees the fear is folklore. Also unsupported: that plant proteins cannot do the job. They can; plant-forward eaters need slightly larger portions and more variety to match the leucine.

**The quiet lever**
Protein is the quietest lever on this shelf: no session required, only architecture. Set the target, anchor every meal, and the strength work from lesson 4 has its raw material for the next forty years.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You can train perfectly and still lose the muscle war at the dinner table. Anabolic resistance raises the price of muscle every decade; 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day, spread across the day, keeps paying it.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Calculate the range now: bodyweight in kilos times 1.6 and times 2.2.$v$, $v$Audit today's breakfast: under 30 g of protein means tomorrow's is the first fix.$v$, $v$Anchor the next meal on the protein before anything else goes on the plate.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$1.6–2.2 g/kg/day when training for the long game; double the old minimum.$v$, $v$Aging muscle needs about 0.4 g/kg per meal to trigger building; spread intake over three or four real doses.$v$, $v$High protein does not harm healthy kidneys in randomised trials.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is anabolic resistance?", "choices": ["An allergy to protein powder", "Refusing steroids", "Aging muscle responding less to each gram of protein, so each meal needs a bigger dose"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Moore 2015: about 0.4 g/kg per meal in older adults against about a quarter of a gram per kilo in the young."}, {"q": "What daily protein range does the evidence support for training adults?", "choices": ["1.6–2.2 g/kg/day", "Over 4 g per kilo", "0.8 g per kilo, the old minimum"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Morton 2018, 49 trials: lean-mass benefit plateaus near 1.6; the old minimum prevents deficiency, not muscle loss."}, {"q": "What does the evidence say about high protein and healthy kidneys?", "choices": ["It reliably damages them", "Randomised trials show no harm to healthy kidney function", "Nobody has studied it"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Devries 2018. Existing kidney disease is a different, clinician-level conversation."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Morton, R. W. et al.", "title": "A systematic review, meta-analysis and meta-regression of the effect of protein supplementation on resistance training-induced gains in muscle mass and strength in healthy adults (Br. J. Sports Med.)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Moore, D. R. et al.", "title": "Protein ingestion to stimulate myofibrillar protein synthesis requires greater relative protein intakes in healthy older versus younger men (J. Gerontol. A)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Devries, M. C. et al.", "title": "Changes in kidney function do not differ between healthy adults consuming higher- compared with lower- or normal-protein diets: a systematic review and meta-analysis (J. Nutr.)", "year": 2018}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$How much protein did today's first meal carry, and what would your 70-year-old muscle have made of it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week at the target, spread across the day. What changed in hunger, and what changed in the gym?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 5, display_order = 5, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'protein-aging-athlete';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Sleep: The Nightly Repair Budget$v$, subtitle = $v$Under six hours shows in the mortality data, and mid-life sleep debt tracks with late-life dementia$v$, summary = $v$Sleep is when the repair you trained for happens. Short sleep tracks with higher mortality and later dementia; the brain-cleaning mechanism is promising science. The protocol costs nothing.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "7–9 h in bed", "frequency": "Every night; a fixed wake time seven days a week", "intensity": "Last caffeine 8–10 h before bed", "prerequisites": "None; the Recovery shelf has the mechanics"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Sleep tied to recovery now and cognition later$v$, $v$Honest tiering: which sleep claims are solid, which are still promising$v$, $v$Three levers and no products$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The dementia and mortality data are observational; short sleep can be a symptom as well as a cause$v$, $v$Shift work breaks the fixed wake time; keep it fixed within each rotation$v$], body_md = $v$**Twice a lever**
You do not adapt during training; you adapt in the repair window afterwards, and the deepest repair window is sleep. On a shelf about the long game, sleep earns its place twice: for what it does this week, and for what the data suggest it does to the brain across decades.

**The mortality data, solid**
Cappuccio et al. (2010) pooled over a million people: habitual sleep under six hours associated with about 12% higher all-cause mortality. Not dramatic, and it applies to a third of adults, every night, for decades. Very long sleep associates with higher mortality too, most likely because illness causes long sleep rather than the reverse; association is not causation, and the tiering here is honest about it.

**The dementia signal, promising**
Sabia et al. (2021) followed 7,959 British civil servants for 25 years: those sleeping six hours or less at 50 to 70 had about 30% higher dementia incidence than seven-hour sleepers, independent of the usual confounders. One study, observational, and 25 years of follow-up makes it hard to dismiss.

**The mechanism, promising, and why the chip**
Xie et al. (2013) showed in mice that during sleep the space between brain cells expands and cerebrospinal fluid flushes metabolic waste, including the amyloid that accumulates in Alzheimer's, at far higher rates than when awake. Elegant, consistent with the human data, and human confirmation is still maturing. A promising mechanism, not a settled fact.

**What the evidence does not support**
Banking sleep in advance. Thriving on five hours; the short-sleeper mutations exist and are rare. Supplements replacing sleep architecture. And alcohol helping you sleep: it fragments the second half of the night and suppresses REM.

**Three levers, no products**
A fixed wake time, seven days a week; the circadian anchor everything hangs on. 7–9 h in bed, counted back from the wake time and defended like a training session; you cannot control sleep, only the window you give it. Last caffeine 8–10 h before bed; the half-life quietly steals deep sleep even when you fall asleep fine. The Recovery shelf owns each of these in full. Track it like training; the evening reflection has the slot. The bar is not perfection, it is protecting the budget most nights for decades.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Every other lever on this shelf, adaptation, muscle, metabolic health, even willpower, runs its repair cycle during sleep. Protecting the window is the cheapest longevity intervention there is.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Set tomorrow's wake time now and count back eight and a half hours; that is tonight's lights-out.$v$, $v$Check the clock at your last caffeine today; inside ten hours of bed, move it earlier tomorrow.$v$, $v$Log hours in bed for a week.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Under six hours of habitual sleep is about 12% higher all-cause mortality across a million people.$v$, $v$Mid-life short sleep tracked with about 30% higher dementia incidence over 25 years.$v$, $v$Fixed wake time, 7–9 h in bed, last caffeine 8–10 h before bed.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Why does this lesson carry a promising chip rather than strong?", "choices": ["The author was unsure", "Sleep research is pseudoscience", "The mortality association is solid; the brain-cleaning mechanism is still maturing in humans"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Million-person mortality data are solid; the waste-clearance mechanism is elegant mouse science with human confirmation in progress."}, {"q": "What did the 25-year Whitehall follow-up find about mid-life sleep?", "choices": ["Six hours or less at 50 to 70 associated with about 30% higher dementia incidence", "More sleep always means less dementia", "Sleep has no long-term effects"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Sabia 2021: persistent short sleep in mid-life tracked with higher dementia rates decades later, independent of the usual confounders."}, {"q": "Which is the first lever of the protocol?", "choices": ["A supplement stack", "A fixed wake time, seven days a week", "Sleeping in at weekends to repay debt"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The clock anchors on the wake time and the morning light; weekend catch-up shifts the anchor and fragments the system."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Cappuccio, F. P., D'Elia, L., Strazzullo, P. & Miller, M. A.", "title": "Sleep duration and all-cause mortality: a systematic review and meta-analysis of prospective studies (Sleep)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Sabia, S. et al.", "title": "Association of sleep duration in middle and old age with incidence of dementia (Nat. Commun.)", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Xie, L. et al.", "title": "Sleep drives metabolite clearance from the adult brain (Science)", "year": 2013}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$How many nights in the last seven were under seven hours, and what did you tell yourself about them?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week with the window defended. What changed first, the mornings or the sessions?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 6, display_order = 6, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'sleep-repair-budget';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Metabolic Health and the Waistline$v$, subtitle = $v$Insulin resistance is upstream of the biggest killers, and it answers to muscle, movement and meals$v$, summary = $v$Heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and a slice of dementia share one upstream driver: insulin resistance. You can screen it with a tape measure and move it within weeks with tools you already have.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 to 15 min walk after the largest meal", "frequency": "Daily; waist measured monthly", "intensity": "Waist under half your height; muscle and Zone 2 from lessons 3 and 4", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$One upstream target instead of four separate disease fears$v$, $v$A tape-measure screen you can do at home today$v$, $v$The post-meal walk, the highest return per minute on the shelf$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The waist rule is a screen, not a diagnosis; blood tests decide$v$, $v$Diabetes on medication: post-meal activity changes glucose fast, so plan it with your clinician$v$], body_md = $v$**One driver**
Heart attack, stroke, type 2 diabetes, much of chronic kidney disease and a share of dementia risk look like separate fates. Metabolically they share an upstream driver: insulin resistance, the cells going gradually deaf to the hormone that manages fuel. The sequence runs quietly for a decade or more. Cells respond less, the pancreas shouts louder, fat accumulates around the organs, inflammation and blood pressure creep up, and glucose finally rises to a diagnosis. By the time a fasting glucose test flags it the process is old. Every stage before the diagnosis is reversible with levers you already own.

**The tape-measure screen**
Visceral fat, the metabolically active fat around the organs, is the visible face of insulin resistance, and the waist tracks it. Ashwell et al. (2012) pooled the screening studies and found waist-to-height ratio beats body mass index for cardiometabolic risk. The rule is simple: keep the waist under half your height. At 180 cm, a waist under 90 cm, measured at the navel, relaxed. No lab, no scale debates, and muscle never inflates the number the way it corrupts the index.

**Three tools you already have**
Muscle, lesson 4: it clears most post-meal glucose, and every strength session builds the sink. Zone 2, lesson 3: easy aerobic work multiplies the mitochondria that burn the fuel, the best-documented exercise tool for insulin sensitivity. The post-meal walk: contracting muscle pulls glucose from the blood without needing much insulin at all. DiPietro et al. (2013) had older adults at risk walk for 15 minutes after each meal and found it controlled 24-hour glucose better than one 45-minute walk, with the after-dinner walk the most effective. Even ten minutes blunts the spike; the Training shelf's lesson on daily movement has the two-minute version.

**What the evidence does not support**
That sugar is uniquely toxic and everything else is safe; a chronic calorie surplus drives visceral fat whatever its source, though liquid sugar makes the surplus effortless. That healthy people need a continuous glucose monitor; a tape and the habits above carry most of the value at none of the cost. And detoxes of any kind; the liver is not waiting for a juice.

**Track it**
Measure the waist monthly, same conditions. It moves slowly, and the direction is the truth about whether the system upstream of four diseases is improving.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Insulin resistance runs silently for a decade before any diagnosis, driving the four biggest causes of lost healthspan. A tape measure screens it; muscle, Zone 2 and the post-meal walk reverse it.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Measure your waist at the navel today and divide by your height; over 0.5 is the signal.$v$, $v$Walk ten minutes after your largest meal today and feel how little it costs.$v$, $v$Put the waist number in your notes with the date; measure again in a month.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$One upstream driver, insulin resistance, feeds heart disease, stroke, diabetes and some dementia risk.$v$, $v$Screen: waist under half your height; the ratio beats body mass index.$v$, $v$Post-meal walks pull glucose into muscle without insulin; 10 to 15 minutes, best after dinner.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is the tape-measure rule?", "choices": ["Body mass index under 25", "Wrist size equals ankle size", "Waist under half your height"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Waist-to-height ratio out-screens the index for cardiometabolic risk and is immune to the muscular-person problem."}, {"q": "Why do post-meal walks work so well?", "choices": ["Contracting muscle pulls glucose from the blood with little insulin needed", "They speed up stomach emptying", "They burn hundreds of calories"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Active muscle takes up glucose through an insulin-light pathway; walking during the post-meal flood blunts the spike at almost no cost."}, {"q": "Which claim does the evidence not support?", "choices": ["Muscle improves insulin sensitivity", "Healthy people need a continuous glucose monitor to manage metabolic health", "Zone 2 builds mitochondria"], "correct": 1, "explain": "For healthy people the tape, the muscle, the Zone 2 and the walk carry most of the value. The gadget is optional; the habits are not."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Ashwell, M., Gunn, P. & Gibson, S.", "title": "Waist-to-height ratio is a better screening tool than waist circumference and BMI for adult cardiometabolic risk factors: systematic review and meta-analysis (Obes. Rev.)", "year": 2012}, {"author": "DiPietro, L., Gribok, A., Stevens, M. S., Hamm, L. F. & Rumpler, W.", "title": "Three 15-min bouts of moderate postmeal walking significantly improves 24-h glycemic control in older people at risk for impaired glucose tolerance (Diabetes Care)", "year": 2013}, {"author": "Reaven, G. M.", "title": "Role of insulin resistance in human disease (Diabetes)", "year": 1988}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is your waist-to-height ratio today, and when did you last know it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A month of post-meal walks and one measurement. Which direction did the number move?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 7, display_order = 7, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'metabolic-health-waistline';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Connection and Purpose: The Soft Lever With Hard Data$v$, subtitle = $v$Isolation carries mortality risk in smoking's class, and your tribe is a longevity protocol$v$, summary = $v$The least marketable longevity lever has some of the hardest data. Social connection and a reason to get up in the morning both predict survival at magnitudes that rival classic risk factors, in observational studies.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "One standing anchor a week", "frequency": "Weekly; purpose reviewed weekly", "intensity": "Train with others where you can; purpose in one sentence", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Community reframed from nice-to-have to a measured lever$v$, $v$Purpose data: measurable protection, not a poster$v$, $v$A weekly protocol; connection is trainable like any capacity$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Observational data throughout; you cannot randomise people into loneliness, which is why the chip says promising$v$, $v$Online contact does not substitute for the real thing in the data$v$], body_md = $v$**The unmarketable lever**
If a pill reduced all-cause mortality as much as strong social ties do, it would be the best-selling drug in history. Instead the lever is free, unmarketable, and mostly ignored by the optimisation crowd.

**The isolation data**
Holt-Lunstad et al. (2010) pooled 148 studies and 308,849 participants: people with stronger social relationships had about 50% higher odds of survival over the study periods, an effect in the same class as quitting smoking and larger than the effects of obesity and inactivity in the same framework. Holt-Lunstad et al. (2015) followed up: loneliness and living alone each associated with roughly 26 to 32% higher mortality. The mechanisms are mundane and powerful: buffered stress physiology, someone who notices when something is off, higher adherence to everything, training included, and more reasons to stay capable.

**The purpose data**
Boyle et al. (2009) followed 1,238 older adults: those reporting a high purpose in life had about 40% lower mortality over five years than those without, after adjusting for depression and disease. Hill and Turiano (2014) replicated the direction across 6,163 adults over 14 years: purpose predicted survival at every age, not only in retirement. The Japanese word ikigai, a reason to get up in the morning, names the same finding; the famous Okinawan stories are observational and confounded, and the prospective purpose studies stand on their own.

**Why promising, not strong**
These are associations. You cannot randomise people into loneliness for a decade. The dose-response patterns, the replication count and the plausible mechanisms make this as strong as observational evidence gets, and the chip rates the evidence for the practice as written. What the evidence does not support: that online parasocial contact substitutes for the real thing, or that purpose must be grand. My grandchildren, my crew, the thing I am building all count. The measure is whether your days feel directed.

**Train it like a capacity**
A standing weekly anchor: one recurring commitment where people expect you, a training crew, a Sunday call, a club. Scheduled beats spontaneous, because spontaneous decays. Train with others when you can; it double-counts, pulling lever 1 or 2 and this one in the same hour, and the tribe in this app exists for exactly that. Name your purpose in one sentence; the coach's long-term goal is where it lives. Review it weekly, because purposeless training eventually loses to friction. The strongest version of this shelf is not done alone. That is what the mortality tables say.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Isolation predicts death at magnitudes that rival smoking, and purpose cuts mortality by about 40% in prospective cohorts. Your tribe and your stated goal are not app features; they are levers.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Put one recurring social-training anchor in the calendar this week: a crew session, a run club, a standing call.$v$, $v$Write your one-sentence reason to stay capable for the next thirty years and store it as the coach's long-term goal.$v$, $v$Train with one other person this week, once.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Stronger social ties: about 50% higher survival odds across 308,849 people.$v$, $v$High purpose in life: about 40% lower mortality over five years, adjusted for depression and disease.$v$, $v$Protocol: one standing weekly anchor, train with others, purpose in one sentence reviewed weekly.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "How large was the survival effect of strong relationships in the 2010 meta-analysis?", "choices": ["Only relevant for the elderly", "Negligible", "About 50% higher odds of survival, in the same class as quitting smoking"], "correct": 2, "explain": "148 studies, 308,849 people: relationship strength predicted survival at magnitudes that rival classic risk factors."}, {"q": "What did Boyle's purpose study find?", "choices": ["About 40% lower mortality over five years in those with a high purpose in life", "Purpose only matters if it is career-related", "Purpose is unmeasurable"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Adjusted for depression and disease. Hill and Turiano replicated the direction across adulthood."}, {"q": "Why does training with your tribe double-count?", "choices": ["You burn twice the calories", "Each session pulls a physical lever and the connection lever in the same hour", "It does not; training should be solitary"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A shared session is fitness or strength work plus a standing social anchor: two levers in one slot."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Holt-Lunstad, J., Smith, T. B. & Layton, J. B.", "title": "Social relationships and mortality risk: a meta-analytic review (PLoS Med.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Holt-Lunstad, J., Smith, T. B., Baker, M., Harris, T. & Stephenson, D.", "title": "Loneliness and social isolation as risk factors for mortality: a meta-analytic review (Perspect. Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Boyle, P. A., Barnes, L. L., Buchman, A. S. & Bennett, D. A.", "title": "Purpose in life is associated with mortality among community-dwelling older persons (Psychosom. Med.)", "year": 2009}, {"author": "Hill, P. L. & Turiano, N. A.", "title": "Purpose in life as a predictor of mortality across adulthood (Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2014}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Who expects you somewhere every week, and what is your one-sentence reason to stay capable?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week with one standing anchor and one shared session. What did the session feel like compared with training alone?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 8, display_order = 8, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'connection-purpose-longevity';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$The Supplement Graveyard$v$, subtitle = $v$Resveratrol, NAD boosters, antioxidant megadoses: what the outcome data say$v$, summary = $v$The longevity supplement industry sells mechanisms, not outcomes. Here lies what the human data buried, the two modest exceptions worth knowing, and the filter that protects your money and attention.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 min once", "frequency": "Before any longevity product", "intensity": "Human outcomes? Which lever? Who profits?", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Named claims checked against outcome data, not mechanism stories$v$, $v$Two honest exceptions where the evidence is real and modest$v$, $v$A reusable filter for every future product$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The negative conclusion is strong; the two exceptions are modest and support levers, they never replace one$v$, $v$Prescribed medicines are not supplements; this lesson is not about them$v$], body_md = $v$**Mechanism is the product**
Every supplement in this graveyard had a beautiful mechanism story, a charismatic scientist and a market. None has human outcome data that survives contact with the levers in lessons 3 to 8. The chip on this lesson is strong because its conclusion rests on a Cochrane meta-analysis and a nine-year cohort, and the conclusion is negative.

**Resveratrol, the red wine molecule**
The story: activate sirtuins, live like a calorie-restricted mouse. The human reality: Semba et al. (2014) measured resveratrol metabolites in 783 older adults in the InCHIANTI cohort over nine years and found no association with mortality, cardiovascular disease, cancer or inflammation markers. The pharmaceutical company that bought the flagship sirtuin business for 720 million dollars later shelved the programme. The mice were interesting. You are not a mouse.

**NAD boosters**
True: NAD declines with age, and the boosters raise blood NAD levels. Also true: raising a biomarker is not an outcome. Human trials to date show changed blood chemistry and little else that is clinically meaningful. No trial shows the boosters extend human healthspan or lifespan; perhaps future ones will. Today you would be paying a premium to change a lab number.

**Antioxidant megadoses, the cautionary headstone**
Here the data went negative. Bjelakovic et al. (2012), the Cochrane review of 78 trials and nearly 297,000 people, found beta-carotene and vitamin E supplementation associated with slightly higher all-cause mortality. Worse for athletes: Ristow et al. (2009) found high-dose vitamins C and E blunted the insulin-sensitivity gains from training. Oxidative stress is part of the signal a session sends; swallowing megadoses to fight it fights your own progress.

**Two modest exceptions**
Creatine monohydrate: the most-studied sports supplement there is, reliably supporting strength and lean mass, lever 2's currency, with early and unsettled work on cognition (Kreider et al. 2017). Cheap, safe, boring; buy the plain monohydrate, every fancier form is marketing. Omega-3: a modest cardiovascular signal in meta-analyses, strongest in people who eat little fish, and fish twice a week likely covers it. Both support levers. Neither replaces one.

**The filter, for ever**
Before any longevity product: is there human outcome data, or only mechanism and biomarkers? Which of the five levers does it pull, and by how much compared with training? Who profits if I believe this? Mechanism plus mouse plus subscription is the industry's whole formula. Lessons 3 to 8 are the counter-formula, and they cost almost nothing.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The supplement industry monetises the gap between mechanism and outcome. Knowing what the human data buried, and the two modest exceptions, protects the money and the attention your real levers need.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Audit the supplement shelf with the filter: human outcomes, which lever, who profits.$v$, $v$Redirect one subscription's monthly cost toward something that pulls a lever: shoes, a gym month, protein-rich food.$v$, $v$If creatine is on the list, check it is plain monohydrate.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Resveratrol: no association with mortality or disease over nine years in humans.$v$, $v$Antioxidant megadoses: slightly higher mortality in the Cochrane review, and blunted training gains.$v$, $v$Creatine and omega-3 are the modest exceptions; supplements support levers and never replace one.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What did the InCHIANTI cohort find about resveratrol in humans?", "choices": ["It worked only in women", "Five extra years of life", "No association with mortality, cardiovascular disease or cancer over nine years"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Semba 2014: measured metabolites in 783 adults, nine years, nothing. The sirtuin story was compelling in mice."}, {"q": "Why are antioxidant megadoses the cautionary headstone?", "choices": ["Slightly higher mortality in the Cochrane review, and they blunt training adaptations", "They are too expensive", "They are merely useless"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Bjelakovic 2012 across nearly 297,000 trial participants; Ristow 2009 on the training gains. Oxidative stress is part of the signal."}, {"q": "The three-question filter for any longevity product?", "choices": ["Price, taste, packaging", "Human outcome data? Which lever, compared with training? Who profits?", "Is it natural, organic, ancient"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Mechanism plus mouse plus subscription is the industry formula. Outcomes, levers and incentives are the counter-formula."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Semba, R. D. et al.", "title": "Resveratrol levels and all-cause mortality in older community-dwelling adults (JAMA Intern. Med.)", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Bjelakovic, G., Nikolova, D., Gluud, L. L., Simonetti, R. G. & Gluud, C.", "title": "Antioxidant supplements for prevention of mortality in healthy participants and patients with various diseases (Cochrane Database Syst. Rev.)", "year": 2012}, {"author": "Ristow, M. et al.", "title": "Antioxidants prevent health-promoting effects of physical exercise in humans (Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA)", "year": 2009}, {"author": "Kreider, R. B. et al.", "title": "International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand: safety and efficacy of creatine supplementation in exercise, sport, and medicine (J. Int. Soc. Sports Nutr.)", "year": 2017}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is on your supplement shelf, and which lever does each one pull?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You ran the filter on the shelf. What went in the bin, and what did the money buy instead?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 9, display_order = 9, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'supplement-graveyard';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Your 100-Year Operating System$v$, subtitle = $v$The whole shelf as one repeatable week, and the only metric that matters: showing up for decades$v$, summary = $v$Nine lessons collapse into one weekly template you can run for the rest of your life. Two strength sessions, a Zone 2 base, one interval session, a protein floor, a sleep window, a waist check and one social anchor.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "6 to 8 h a week", "frequency": "Weekly, for decades", "intensity": "2 strength · 2–4 Zone 2 · 1 interval · protein floor · sleep window · post-meal walk · one social anchor", "prerequisites": "Lessons 1 to 9"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$The entire shelf on one page$v$, $v$Every element mapped to an existing check-in habit$v$, $v$Built to survive bad weeks, not demand perfect ones$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Installing the full template from zero fails; start at the floor and grow$v$, $v$The template is a container; the evidence for each line sits in its lesson$v$], body_md = $v$**The week**
Nine lessons, five levers, decades of mortality data, and none of it matters unless it compresses into something you can run on an ordinary Tuesday for thirty years. Here it is.

Training, levers 1 and 2: two full-body strength sessions across the six patterns, lesson 4. Zone 2 at 2–4 sessions of 45–60 minutes at conversational pace, lesson 3. One 4×4 interval session, lesson 3.

Daily architecture, levers 3 and 4: the protein floor at 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day, spread over three or four meals, lesson 5. The sleep window: a fixed wake time, 7–9 h in bed, last caffeine 8–10 h before bed, lesson 6. A walk after the largest meal, lesson 7.

Weekly anchors, lever 5 and measurement: one standing social-training commitment, and the tribe session counts double, lesson 8. Monthly, the waist-to-height check, under 0.5, lesson 7. Never, money on anything that fails the graveyard filter, lesson 9.

That is the whole system, six to eight hours a week, less time than most people spend reading about longevity.

**Make it survive contact with life**
A system for decades must absorb bad weeks without collapsing. Two rules. The floor rule: on overwhelmed weeks the system shrinks instead of stopping, to one strength session, one Zone 2, protein at every meal and the sleep window. The floor keeps the identity alive, and identity, as the Inner Work shelf teaches, is what carries behaviour across years. Never miss twice: one missed day is life, two is the start of a new trajectory, which is exactly what the check-in streak measures. The app's core loop is the longevity loop.

**Start where you are**
If you train zero times a week, do not install the full template on Monday. Li et al. (2018) and the fitness cohorts agree that the biggest gains come from the first steps; unfit to somewhat fit is the largest jump on the whole curve. One strength session and two walks is a magnificent week one.

**The long view**
The last decade of your life is being negotiated this decade, in ordinary weeks like the coming one. The tools are unglamorous: a barbell, a conversational run, a bedtime, a protein target, a crew, a tape measure. The evidence behind them embarrasses every product ever sold against ageing. Run the week. Then run it again.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$A longevity practice only works at the scale of decades, so it must be simple enough to repeat for ever and flexible enough to survive real life. This template is the whole shelf in one week.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Lay this week's template into the calendar now: anchors first, then fill.$v$, $v$Define your personal floor, the shrunken week you can always run, and write it where you plan.$v$, $v$If you are starting from zero: one strength session and two walks this week, nothing more.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$The week: two strength, 2–4 Zone 2, one interval, protein floor, sleep window, social anchor.$v$, $v$The floor rule: bad weeks shrink the system, never stop it; identity survives on the floor.$v$, $v$Never miss twice; the check-in streak is the longevity metric.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is the floor rule?", "choices": ["Always train to failure", "Train on the floor for core strength", "On overwhelmed weeks the system shrinks to a minimum version instead of stopping"], "correct": 2, "explain": "One strength session, one Zone 2, protein, the sleep window. The floor keeps the identity and the streak alive."}, {"q": "Where do the biggest mortality gains on the fitness curve come from?", "choices": ["Unfit to somewhat fit, the first steps", "Gains are evenly spread", "Very fit to elite"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The steepest part of the curve is at the bottom. Starting small is the highest-return move available."}, {"q": "What single behaviour metric does this shelf reduce to?", "choices": ["Your one-rep maximum", "Showing up repeatedly for decades, the streak", "Your supplement count"], "correct": 1, "explain": "